{
  "items": [
    {
      "id": "a01",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 2,
      "estimated_minutes": 2,
      "reason": "The comparison centers directly on the breadth of “in connection with or relating to,” the words construed in d01. The separate “Hotei/Casinos” typo objection can be set aside without investigating it."
    },
    {
      "id": "a02",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 2,
      "estimated_minutes": 2,
      "reason": "The description clearly identifies the boundary between authorized promotion or broadcasting and a separate entertainment product. Comparing that boundary with d02 is direct, and the exclusive-license issue in d03 is readily distinguishable."
    },
    {
      "id": "a03",
      "difficulty": "hard",
      "score": 7,
      "estimated_minutes": 15,
      "reason": "The reviewer must test the asserted conflict against Sections 2(a) and 3(a), then distinguish exclusivity over Hotel Marks for casino-free hotels from exclusivity against other branded ventures. The difficult step is deciding whether this particular alleged conflict names the problem the court addressed in rejecting the broader noncompetition reading."
    },
    {
      "id": "a04",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 2,
      "estimated_minutes": 2,
      "reason": "The description targets the measurability of the Las Vegas quality baseline, while d04 concerns whether “nature and quality” reaches content and characteristics rather than merely excellence. Those questions are plainly separable without establishing whether the baseline was actually recorded."
    },
    {
      "id": "a05",
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "score": 6,
      "estimated_minutes": 10,
      "reason": "The reviewer must check the assertion that the contract never identifies quality-control provisions against Section 4 and the arbitration cross-reference. The remaining comparison requires distinguishing uncertainty about which provisions trigger arbitration from d05’s dispute over what kinds of objections count as quality disputes; the “proscribed” objection is separate."
    },
    {
      "id": "a06",
      "difficulty": "hard",
      "score": 8,
      "estimated_minutes": 18,
      "reason": "The reviewer must trace the exclusion back through Section 5(a) and determine what the description means by a mixed dispute involving “trademark validity.” The difficult comparison is between genuinely combined quality-and-validity claims and the court’s treatment of quality allegations pleaded as infringement or termination claims."
    },
    {
      "id": "a07",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 2,
      "estimated_minutes": 2,
      "reason": "The absence of a benchmark for measuring “best efforts” is explicit in both the description and the challenge discussed in d07. The additional references to cost ceilings and mutual obligations do not require a separate factual inquiry to compare that central complaint."
    },
    {
      "id": "a08",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 1,
      "estimated_minutes": 1,
      "reason": "The description expressly targets “when practical and appropriate,” whereas d08 turns on the competing meanings of “locations.” The different words and interpretive questions are apparent immediately."
    },
    {
      "id": "a09",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 2,
      "estimated_minutes": 2,
      "reason": "The table-tent approval language is readily separated from d08’s geographic ambiguity in the cross-promotion duty. Whether “Licensor” is actually a mechanical error need not be resolved to make that comparison."
    },
    {
      "id": "a10",
      "difficulty": "hard",
      "score": 9,
      "estimated_minutes": 25,
      "reason": "The reviewer must untangle the asserted conflict among the facilities definition, territorial exclusivity, the website exception, and the Internet-sales rules. Even after checking those links, the description leaves uncertain whether its general complaint about competing web rights reaches d09’s specific exclusive-domain-registration problem or instead identifies a different boundary dispute."
    },
    {
      "id": "a11",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 3,
      "estimated_minutes": 3,
      "reason": "One additional look distinguishes the proposed wholesale-sublicense conflict with Section 9(c) from d12’s authorization of sublicensed use at Tulsa and Albuquerque. The reviewer need not settle whether a wholesale sublicense actually circumvents the first-offer right to distinguish those issues."
    }
  ]
}
